  "assessment_summary": "This document is a methodological guide produced by Founders Pledge to standardize the discounts applied to RCT effect sizes in cost-effectiveness analyses (CEAs) for global health and development charities. It is structured around three key questions: (1) How large should Type M (magnitude) errors be expected to be? (2) What is the possibility of Type S (sign) errors? (3) Will RCT results generalize to new areas? The document separates discounts into internal replicability and external validity components, drawing on the Gelman and Carlin (2014) framework for Type S and Type M errors.\n\nStrengths: The document addresses an important practical problem—how to systematically adjust effect sizes from published studies for use in CEAs—that is highly relevant to effective altruism and evidence-based philanthropy. The separation of internal replicability from external validity is sensible and well-motivated. The author draws on a reasonable range of relevant literature, including replication studies (Open Science Collaboration 2015, Camerer et al. 2016, 2018), meta-scientific evidence (Fanelli et al. 2017, Bartoš et al. 2022), and development economics-specific work (Coville & Vivalt 2017, Vivalt 2020). The inclusion of practical guidance sections with step-by-step instructions, a worked example, and references to tools like the retrodesign R package makes the document potentially useful for practitioners. The acknowledgment that the guidelines are a work in progress and the invitation for feedback reflect appropriate epistemic humility.\n\nWeaknesses and concerns: (1) The document is essentially a practical guide rather than original research. It synthesizes existing findings but does not produce new empirical evidence, develop new methods, or formally test whether the proposed framework improves CEA accuracy relative to alternatives. There is no validation of the proposed approach—no back-testing or simulation to assess whether following these guidelines produces better-calibrated predictions than, say, GiveWell's existing approach. (2) The multiplicative framework for combining discount factors (e.g., 1/(1.2 × 1.1 × 1.15) = 0.66) is presented without rigorous justification. This assumes that the various sources of bias compound multiplicatively, which may not be correct—some biases may overlap or interact in non-multiplicative ways. More importantly, the individual bias estimates (e.g., '10% inflated due to methodological errors,' '15% inflated due to researcher bias') are themselves highly subjective, and the guide provides little concrete guidance on how to arrive at these numbers beyond general heuristics. (3) The treatment of external validity is notably thinner than internal replicability, as the author acknowledges. The claim that external validity may matter more than internal replicability is intriguing but remains largely unsubstantiated. The Vivalt (2020) finding that effect sizes differ by ~99% across contexts is cited but the guidance for how to use this information is quite vague—essentially suggesting qualitative assessment of necessary conditions without a systematic quantitative framework. (4) Several quantitative claims deserve more careful treatment. For instance, the suggestion that 'many RCTs are likely to need a 50-60% Type M discount' is presented as a central estimate but this averages across very different fields (psychology, economics, medicine) and study types, without careful consideration of how development economics RCTs differ from the reference studies. The Coville & Vivalt (2017) estimate of 69% adjustment is the most directly relevant but draws from a database dominated by well-studied interventions. (5) The document sometimes conflates different concepts. For example, the discussion of Type S errors mixes false positives (the effect doesn't exist at all) with sign errors (the effect exists but in the opposite direction). The Ioannidis (2005) framework is about false positive rates, not Type S errors per se. (6) Table 1, which is central to the guidance, mixes very different types of evidence—actual replication studies (Open Science Collaboration, Camerer et al.) with Bayesian bias correction methods (Coville & Vivalt, Bartoš et al.)—without adequately discussing why these produce different estimates or which is most relevant. The author expresses skepticism about the Bartoš et al. psychology result (70% adjustment) but not about other entries. (7) The worked example, while illustrative, reveals the significant subjectivity involved. Many of the specific discount numbers (e.g., '15% for researcher bias,' '20% for proximity to healthcare centers') appear to be educated guesses rather than empirically grounded estimates. This raises questions about whether the framework actually reduces subjectivity relative to the status quo, or merely structures it differently. (8) The treatment of quasi-experimental methods in the appendix is very brief and incomplete, as acknowledged. The claim that quasi-experimental estimates do not systematically differ from RCT estimates (citing Chaplin et al. 2018 and Waddington et al. 2022) deserves more nuance—the absence of systematic bias does not mean individual quasi-experimental studies are equally reliable. (9) There are some internal inconsistencies: the document states the 50-60% discount applies to 'many RCTs' but the worked example yields an 82% discount, suggesting the typical case may be very different from the examples the guide is designed for. (10) The document does not adequately address the question of how these discounts should interact with uncertainty—should CEAs use point estimates with discounts, or would distributional approaches (e.g., modeling uncertainty in the true effect size) be more appropriate?",
